  { t: 'sci',
    q: ['烹調新鮮蔬菜時宜「大火快炒」或「少水快煮」，主要為了保留哪一類營養素？',
      'Fresh vegetables are best stir-fried quickly over high heat or cooked briefly in little water. Which nutrients does this mainly preserve?'],
    o: [['水溶性維生素，例如維生素 C 及維生素 B 雜', 'water-soluble vitamins such as vitamin C and the B group'],
      ['脂肪及其所攜帶的脂溶性成分', 'fat and the fat-soluble substances it carries'],
      ['膳食纖維及其保水的能力', 'dietary fibre and its capacity to hold water'],
      ['礦物質之中的鈣質及其吸收率', 'the calcium among the minerals, and its absorption']],
    e: ['水溶性維生素怕熱、怕水、怕久煮：長時間浸煮會令它們溶入水中隨湯棄去，高溫久煮則會令其分解。故此縮短加熱時間、減少用水，甚至留起煮菜水作湯底，都能減少損失。至於膳食纖維及礦物質，性質相對穩定，一般烹調不會令它們大量流失。',
      'Water-soluble vitamins are vulnerable to heat, to water and to long cooking: soaking or boiling for a long time leaches them into the water, which is then poured away, and prolonged high heat breaks them down. Shortening the cooking, using less water, or keeping the cooking water for stock all reduce the loss. Dietary fibre and minerals, by contrast, are relatively stable and are not lost in quantity in ordinary cooking.'],
  },
  { t: 'sci',
    q: ['乳酪、豉油、麵豉等發酵食品的製作，主要依靠甚麼？',
      'The making of fermented foods such as yoghurt, soy sauce and bean paste depends mainly on what?'],
    o: [['特定微生物的活動', 'the activity of particular micro-organisms'],
      ['長時間的高溫消毒', 'prolonged sterilisation at high temperature'],
      ['加入化學防腐劑', 'the addition of chemical preservatives'],
      ['持續的冷凍處理', 'continuous freezing treatment']],
    e: ['發酵是由選定的細菌、酵母或霉菌分解食物中的糖或蛋白質，產生酸、酒精及各種風味物質。所產生的酸能同時抑制腐敗菌及致病菌，故發酵既造風味，亦是一種古老的保存方法。留意這與「食物變壞」的分別不在於有沒有微生物，而在於微生物是否受控、是哪一種 —— 前者是刻意接種並控制條件，後者是任由環境中的雜菌生長。',
      'Fermentation uses selected bacteria, yeasts or moulds to break down the sugars or proteins in a food, producing acids, alcohol and a range of flavour compounds. The acid produced also suppresses spoilage and pathogenic organisms, so fermentation both creates flavour and serves as an ancient method of preservation. Note that what separates this from spoilage is not the presence of micro-organisms but which ones and whether they are controlled — deliberate inoculation under managed conditions on one side, whatever happens to be in the air on the other.'],
  },
  { t: 'sci',
    q: ['油炸食物時若油溫過低，最可能出現甚麼情況？',
      'What is most likely to happen if the oil is not hot enough when deep-frying?'],
    o: [['食物吸收較多油分，成品油膩而不鬆脆', 'the food takes up more oil and turns out greasy rather than crisp'],
      ['食物外層迅速焦黑，內部仍未熟透', 'the outside browns and burns quickly while the inside stays raw'],
      ['油會立即大量冒煙並有起火危險', 'the oil immediately smokes heavily and risks catching fire'],
      ['食物完全不會變色，亦不會被加熱', 'the food neither browns nor heats up at all']],
    e: ['油溫足夠時，食物表面的水分會迅速汽化，形成一層向外的蒸氣，阻止油分大量滲入，同時令表層脫水變脆。油溫不足則水分蒸發緩慢，油有時間滲入食物內部，成品因而油膩。相反的問題 —— 外焦內生、油冒煙起火 —— 屬油溫【過高】的徵狀，兩者的處理方法剛好相反，不可混淆。',
      'When the oil is hot enough, moisture at the surface of the food turns to steam at once, and that outward flow of steam keeps oil from soaking in while the surface dries and crisps. If the oil is too cool the moisture leaves slowly, oil has time to penetrate, and the result is greasy. The opposite problems — a burnt outside with a raw centre, or oil smoking and catching fire — are signs of oil that is too hot, and the remedies are opposite, so the two must not be confused.'],
  },
  { t: 'sci',
    q: ['把蔬菜灼水後立即放入冰水中冷卻（過冷河），主要目的是甚麼？',
      'Vegetables are often plunged into iced water immediately after blanching. What is the main purpose?'],
    o: [['迅速停止加熱，保住顏色與爽脆的質感', 'to stop the cooking at once, keeping the colour and the crisp texture'],
      ['讓蔬菜吸收更多水分，以增加重量', 'to let the vegetables take up more water and gain weight'],
      ['把蔬菜表面的農藥殘餘完全洗走', 'to wash off every trace of pesticide residue from the surface'],
      ['提高蔬菜所含的維生素的總含量', 'to raise the total vitamin content of the vegetables']],
    e: ['離水之後的蔬菜仍有餘熱，若任其自然冷卻，加熱其實並未停止，顏色會由翠綠轉為黃綠，質感亦會變得軟身。冰水令中心溫度迅速下降，把加熱鎖定在恰好的一刻。留意烹調過程只會令營養素減少或保持，不可能憑空增加；而農藥殘餘應在烹調【之前】以流動清水徹底沖洗處理。',
      'Vegetables carry residual heat after they leave the pot, and if left to cool by themselves the cooking does not actually stop: the colour shifts from bright green towards yellow-green and the texture softens. Iced water brings the core temperature down fast, fixing the cooking at exactly the right moment. Note that cooking can only reduce or preserve nutrients, never create them, and that pesticide residues are dealt with before cooking, by thorough washing under running water.'],
  },
  { t: 'sci',
    q: ['食物的水分含量高，對其保存有甚麼影響？',
      'How does a high moisture content affect how well a food keeps?'],
    o: [['微生物較易生長，食物較易腐壞', 'micro-organisms grow more readily, so the food spoils sooner'],
      ['水分能隔絕空氣，食物反而較耐存放', 'the water seals out air, so the food actually keeps longer'],
      ['對保存沒有影響，只影響食物的口感', 'it has no effect on keeping quality, only on texture'],
      ['水分越高，食物內酵素的活動越慢', 'the more water there is, the slower the food’s enzymes work']],
    e: ['微生物生長需要水，而且需要的是可供利用的「自由水」。乾製、醃製及加糖之所以能保存食物，原理相同：不是把微生物殺死，而是把水分抽走或以鹽、糖把水「綁住」，令微生物無水可用。酵素活動同樣需要水，水分越高活動越快，故高水分食物的自然變質亦較迅速。',
      'Micro-organisms need water to grow, and specifically the free water available to them. Drying, salting and sugaring all preserve food on the same principle: not by killing the organisms but by removing the water, or by binding it up with salt or sugar so that none is available. Enzymes likewise need water and work faster when there is more of it, so foods high in moisture also deteriorate on their own more quickly.'],
  },

  // ── 食物安全 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  { t: 'safe',
    q: ['預先包裝食物上的「此日期前最佳」與「此日期前食用」兩種標示，分別在哪裡？',
      'Prepackaged foods carry either a “best before” date or a “use by” date. How do the two differ?'],
    o: [['「此日期前食用」關乎食用安全，「此日期前最佳」關乎品質', '“use by” concerns safety, while “best before” concerns quality'],
      ['兩者意思完全相同，只是不同廠商的寫法', 'the two mean exactly the same thing and are merely different house styles'],
      ['「此日期前最佳」關乎安全，過期即有中毒風險', '“best before” concerns safety, and food is a poisoning risk once it passes'],
      ['兩者都只是建議，過期後一律仍可安全食用', 'both are only suggestions, and food past either date is always still safe']],
    e: ['「此日期前食用」用於容易腐壞、過期後可能危害健康的食物，例如冷藏肉類及即食沙律，過了這個日期就不應食用，即使外觀氣味正常。「此日期前最佳」則指品質最佳的期限，過期後可能風味、口感或營養有所下降，但在包裝完好及貯存得宜的前提下未必不安全。把兩者對調，是實際生活中最容易出事的一種混淆。',
      'A “use by” date is used on foods that spoil readily and may become unsafe, such as chilled meat and ready-to-eat salads; past that date the food should not be eaten, even if it looks and smells normal. A “best before” date marks the end of peak quality: after it the flavour, texture or nutrient content may have declined, but the food is not necessarily unsafe if the packaging is intact and storage has been proper. Swapping the two round is the confusion most likely to cause harm in practice.'],
  },
  { t: 'safe',
    q: ['家用雪櫃的冷藏格（非冷凍格），溫度一般應保持在甚麼範圍？',
      'At what temperature should the chill compartment of a domestic refrigerator — not the freezer — be kept?'],
    o: [['攝氏 4 度或以下', '4 °C or below'],
      ['攝氏 10 度或以下', '10 °C or below'],
      ['攝氏零下 18 度或以下', '−18 °C or below'],
      ['攝氏 15 至 20 度', 'between 15 °C and 20 °C']],
    e: ['細菌繁殖最快的溫度範圍大約由攝氏 4 度至 60 度，故冷藏格必須保持在攝氏 4 度或以下，把繁殖速度壓下來。留意攝氏零下 18 度是【冷凍格】的標準，兩者不可混淆：冷藏只是減慢細菌繁殖，冷凍則使其近乎停止，但兩者都不能把已有的細菌殺死。此外雪櫃不宜塞得太滿，否則冷空氣無法流通，實際溫度會高於設定。',
      'Bacteria multiply fastest between about 4 °C and 60 °C, so the chill compartment must be held at 4 °C or below to keep that multiplication down. Note that −18 °C is the standard for the freezer, and the two must not be confused: chilling slows bacterial growth while freezing all but halts it, but neither kills the bacteria already present. A refrigerator should also not be overpacked, or cold air cannot circulate and the real temperature rises above the setting.'],
  },
  { t: 'safe',
    q: ['已切開的西瓜或已開封的飲品，應該怎樣處理？',
      'How should a cut watermelon or an opened drink be handled?'],
    o: [['加蓋或包好後立即冷藏，並在短時間內食用完', 'cover it, refrigerate it at once and finish it within a short time'],
      ['放在室溫陰涼處即可，數天之內食用完', 'keep it somewhere cool at room temperature and finish it within a few days'],
      ['加蓋後放回原處，可一直存放至包裝上的到期日', 'cover it, return it to where it was and keep it until the date on the pack'],
      ['放入冷凍格結成冰塊，需要時解凍再食用', 'freeze it solid and thaw it again whenever it is wanted']],
    e: ['完整的果皮及密封的包裝本身就是屏障；一旦切開或開封，食物表面即暴露於空氣、刀具及手部的微生物之下，包裝上原印的到期日亦隨即失效 —— 那個日期是為【未開封】的狀態而定的。故此開封後應立即冷藏，並按食物種類在一至兩日內食用完，而非依賴原有標示。',
      'An intact rind or a sealed pack is itself a barrier; once the food is cut or opened, its surface is exposed to micro-organisms from the air, the knife and the hands, and the printed date no longer applies — that date was set for the unopened state. So refrigerate immediately after opening and finish within a day or two depending on the food, rather than relying on the original marking.'],
  },
  { t: 'safe',
    q: ['以下關於清洗蔬果的說法，哪一項正確？',
      'Which of the following statements about washing fruit and vegetables is correct?'],
    o: [['應以流動清水徹底沖洗，必要時去皮，以減少泥土及殘餘', 'wash thoroughly under running water, and peel where appropriate, to reduce soil and residues'],
      ['應以洗潔精浸洗，因為洗潔精可以分解表面的農藥', 'soak in dishwashing detergent, because detergent breaks down surface pesticides'],
      ['只要是有機種植的蔬果，就完全不需要清洗', 'organically grown produce needs no washing at all'],
      ['應以熱水浸泡，因為熱水可去除所有農藥殘餘', 'soak in hot water, because hot water removes all pesticide residues']],
    e: ['流動清水的沖刷力量能帶走泥土、微生物及部分表面殘餘，必要時可用刷洗或去皮進一步減少。洗潔精並非為食用而設，本身可能殘留在蔬果表面，反而構成新的問題。有機種植只是不使用某些合成農藥，並不代表沒有泥土及環境中的微生物。至於熱水浸泡，除了未必更有效，還會令蔬菜變軟並流失水溶性維生素。',
      'The scrubbing action of running water carries away soil, micro-organisms and some surface residue, and brushing or peeling reduces it further where appropriate. Dishwashing detergent is not made to be eaten and may itself remain on the surface, creating a new problem. Organic growing means only that certain synthetic pesticides were not used; it does not remove soil or the micro-organisms present in any field. Soaking in hot water is no more effective and additionally softens vegetables and leaches out water-soluble vitamins.'],
  },
  { t: 'safe',
    q: ['在餐廳用膳後把剩餘的食物打包帶回家，安全的做法是甚麼？',
      'What is the safe way to handle restaurant leftovers taken home?'],
    o: [['盡快放入雪櫃冷藏，翻熱時必須徹底熱透', 'refrigerate as soon as possible, and reheat until piping hot right through'],
      ['放在室溫過夜待完全放涼，第二天再翻熱', 'leave at room temperature overnight to cool fully, then reheat next day'],
      ['直接放入雪櫃，翻熱時稍為加溫便可食用', 'put it straight in the refrigerator and warm it only slightly before eating'],
      ['只要外觀及氣味正常，可以不必翻熱直接食用', 'if it looks and smells normal, eat it cold without reheating']],
    e: ['食物在攝氏 4 度至 60 度之間停留越久，細菌繁殖越多，故關鍵是縮短這段時間 —— 打包後盡快冷藏，不要等到完全放涼。翻熱則須徹底熱透至中心溫度達攝氏 75 度或以上，稍為加溫只會把食物帶進最適合細菌繁殖的溫度範圍，比不加熱更危險。另須留意部分細菌所產生的毒素耐熱，翻熱亦不能消除，所以及早冷藏比事後補救重要。',
      'The longer food sits between 4 °C and 60 °C the more bacteria multiply, so the point is to shorten that period — refrigerate as soon as it is home rather than waiting for it to cool completely. Reheating must take the centre of the food to 75 °C or above; warming it only slightly brings it into precisely the range where bacteria grow best, which is worse than not heating it at all. Note too that the toxins some bacteria produce are heat-stable and are not destroyed by reheating, so chilling promptly matters more than any later remedy.'],
  },

  // ── 纖維與布料 ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  { t: 'fibre',
    q: ['纖維一般分為天然纖維與人造纖維兩大類。以下哪一種屬於天然纖維？',
      'Fibres are broadly divided into natural and man-made. Which of the following is a natural fibre?'],
    o: [['羊毛', 'wool'], ['尼龍', 'nylon'], ['聚酯纖維', 'polyester'], ['腈綸', 'acrylic']],
    e: ['天然纖維直接取自動物或植物：動物性的有羊毛與蠶絲，植物性的有棉與麻。尼龍、聚酯纖維及腈綸均由石油化工原料聚合而成，屬合成纖維。另有一類「再生纖維」（如人造絲）以天然纖維素為原料經化學處理製成，來源天然而製法人造，歸入人造纖維一類 —— 這一類最容易被錯誤歸邊。',
      'Natural fibres are taken directly from animals or plants: wool and silk from animals, cotton and linen from plants. Nylon, polyester and acrylic are all polymerised from petrochemical feedstocks and are synthetic. A further group, the regenerated fibres such as rayon, is made by chemically treating natural cellulose — natural in origin but manufactured in process — and is classed as man-made; this is the group most often placed on the wrong side.'],
  },
  { t: 'fibre',
    q: ['棉纖維取自植物的哪一部分？',
      'Which part of the plant does cotton fibre come from?'],
    o: [['種子上的絨毛', 'the fibres growing on the seed'],
      ['植物的莖部', 'the stem of the plant'],
      ['植物的葉部', 'the leaves of the plant'],
      ['植物的根部', 'the roots of the plant']],
    e: ['棉是包裹在棉籽外的絨毛，屬種子纖維；亞麻及苧麻則取自植物莖部的韌皮，屬莖纖維。兩者的結構分別解釋了它們的手感：棉纖維中空而帶天然扭曲，故柔軟保暖；麻纖維長而直、細胞壁厚，故挺身、涼快而易皺。把「棉」與「麻」的來源對調，是本課常見的錯。',
      'Cotton is the down surrounding the cotton seed and is therefore a seed fibre, while flax and ramie come from the bast of the plant stem and are stem fibres. The difference in structure explains how each feels: the cotton fibre is hollow with a natural twist, hence soft and warm; the bast fibre is long, straight and thick-walled, hence crisp, cool and prone to creasing. Swapping the origins of cotton and linen is a common slip here.'],
  },
  { t: 'fibre',
    q: ['羊毛衣物若用熱水洗滌並用力搓揉，最可能出現甚麼問題？',
      'What is most likely to happen to a woollen garment washed in hot water and rubbed vigorously?'],
    o: [['出現氈化，衣物變厚變硬並縮小', 'it felts, becoming thicker, harder and smaller'],
      ['顏色變得比原來更加鮮艷', 'the colour becomes brighter than it was'],
      ['纖維變得比原來更有彈性', 'the fibres become more elastic than they were'],
      ['衣物明顯伸長並變得寬闊', 'the garment stretches noticeably longer and wider']],
    e: ['羊毛纖維表面覆有一層互相重疊的鱗片，遇熱、遇水再加上機械摩擦，鱗片會互相卡緊而不能復位，纖維因而糾纏收緊，這個不可逆的過程稱為氈化。故羊毛宜用溫水或冷水、輕手手洗或用羊毛程序，並平放晾乾以免因自身重量拉長。一旦氈化，無法還原。',
      'The wool fibre is covered in overlapping scales; heat and water together with mechanical rubbing cause those scales to lock against one another and fail to slide back, so the fibres tangle and tighten. This process, called felting, is irreversible. Wool should therefore be washed in warm or cool water, by hand with a light touch or on a wool programme, and dried flat so that its own weight does not stretch it. Once felted, it cannot be restored.'],
  },
  { t: 'fibre',
    q: ['衣物洗滌標籤上的符號中，一個盆狀的圖案代表甚麼？',
      'On a garment care label, what does the symbol shaped like a tub indicate?'],
    o: [['水洗的方式，包括可否水洗及水溫上限', 'washing: whether it may be washed and the maximum water temperature'],
      ['熨燙的方式，包括熨斗的溫度上限', 'ironing: the maximum temperature of the iron'],
      ['乾洗的方式，以及可用的溶劑種類', 'dry cleaning: and which solvents may be used'],
      ['漂白的處理，以及可用的漂白劑種類', 'bleaching: and which bleaching agents may be used']],
    e: ['洗滌標籤的五個基本符號各有形狀，記住形狀就不必逐個查：盆代表水洗，三角形代表漂白，正方形代表乾燥，熨斗代表熨燙，圓形代表專業護理即乾洗。符號上的數字表示溫度上限，符號被打上交叉則表示不可作該項處理。選購及護理衣物時先看標籤，可避免一次洗壞。',
      'The five basic care symbols each have their own shape, and remembering the shapes saves looking them up: a tub for washing, a triangle for bleaching, a square for drying, an iron for ironing and a circle for professional care, that is dry cleaning. A number inside a symbol gives the maximum temperature, and a cross through a symbol means that treatment must not be used. Reading the label before buying and before washing prevents ruining a garment in one go.'],
  },

  // ── 服裝與時裝 ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  { t: 'fash',
    q: ['服裝設計的基本元素包括線條、顏色、質感，以及以下哪一項？',
      'The basic elements of clothing design include line, colour, texture and which of the following?'],
    o: [['形狀與輪廓', 'shape and silhouette'],
      ['售價與折扣', 'price and discount'],
      ['品牌與商標', 'brand and logo'],
      ['產地與工廠', 'country of origin and factory']],
    e: ['設計元素是設計師可以直接操控、並構成視覺效果的成分：線條引導視線，顏色影響情緒與視覺重量，質感決定光影與手感，形狀與輪廓則決定衣服在人體上呈現的整體外形。售價、品牌及產地屬市場及生產的範疇，會影響消費決定，卻不是構成外觀的設計元素。答設計題時把兩者分清，論述才會準確。',
